{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>From the elohei the peoples</b>, meaning: he will come with an argument to prove from what is before him — from the elohei the peoples that are around you — for the peoples surrounding Israel will see them encamped in tranquility and great success, and they will say to him to resemble what is before him. Or he will come with an argument to make a great proof, and that is when there is idolatry that has spread throughout the world, so that all the world or most of it worships it, as has happened in these times, when the kingdom of Edom all of them worship, etc., and this is a major pillar. And according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Yoma 10a), who said that the wicked kingdom is destined to spread throughout the entire world — behold, one idolatry [exists] throughout the entire world from one end of the earth to the other, and this incitement carries with it an argument to prove that it has substance, heaven forbid. Nevertheless it says ״לֹא תֹאבֶה לוֹ״ and so forth — meaning, the Torah gives a person counsel by which he may escape from it. One [interpretation]: ״לֹא תֹאבֶה לוֹ״, meaning, in the manner of what it says (Proverbs 1:10) ״בְּנִי אִם יְפַתּוּךָ חַטָּאִים אַל תֹּבֵא״ — meaning, do not enter into arguments with him, but rather a negation of desire without argument. And see what I explained on the verse ״וְלֹא אָבָה״ (Deuteronomy 2:30), and he will be precluded from hearing his words further in this matter. And the reason is that if one enters into arguments with him, the persistence of contemplation will give birth to the disposition of evil in the person."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:17:08.031923618Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}